`'use client'` declares a boundary in the module graph; it does not mean the component never renders on a server. In the App Router, pages and layouts are Server Components by default. A Client Component is needed for state that changes in the browser, Effects, event handlers, or browser APIs. Its imports become part of the client graph, so put the directive as low as practical to limit JavaScript. Props crossing from server to client must be serializable, except that a Server Function can cross as a framework-managed reference. A Server Component can still be passed as rendered `children` without importing its code into the client graph.

```tsx caption="The directive marks the module-graph boundary above imports."
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';
```

Both let React treat work as non-urgent, but they mark different things. `useTransition` wraps state updates, returns `isPending`, and is useful for navigation or an update that can suspend while keeping already revealed content visible. `useDeferredValue` creates a lagging version of a value, so an urgent input can update immediately while a slow results view continues using the previous value until the new one is ready. Neither makes expensive JavaScript disappear, and neither supplies data caching. Use the one matching the owner of the decision: the producer marks an update with a transition; a consumer can defer a value it receives.

```tsx caption="Mark navigation state as non-urgent while retaining a pending indicator."
const [isPending, startTransition] = useTransition();
startTransition(() => setRoute(nextRoute));
```
